 three types of scope in JavaScript: global scope, function scope, and block scope.",href:"/en/swe/what-is-scope-and-scope-chain"},{title:"What is JavaScript Closures?",date:"2023-02-13",lastmod:"2023-02-13",toc:"javascript",lang:"en",fileName:"what-is-closure",filePath:"src/pages/en/swe/what-is-closure.md",fullPath:"/en/swe/what-is-closure",subtoc:"variable",order:2,images:"https://www.explainthis.io/api/og?title=What is JavaScript Closures?",summary:"Closures are a fundamental concept in programming languages, and their use is widespread in JavaScript. Many important functions in JavaScript libraries utilize closures, including the useState function in the React library, which is implemented using closures. Understanding not only what closures are, but also their practical applications, is crucial to effectively leveraging their power.",href:"/en/swe/what-is-closure"},{title:"Explain What Hoisting Is in JavaScript",date:"2023-02-13",lastmod:"2023-02-13",toc:"javascript",lang:"en",fileName:"hoisting",filePath:"src/pages/en/swe/hoisting.md",fullPath:"/en/swe/hoisting",subtoc:"variable",order:3,images:"https://www.explainthis.io/api/og?title=Explain What Hoisting Is in JavaScript",summary:"In this article, you will learn about hoisting in JavaScript, including how it works with different variable declarations like var, let, and function declarations. The article also covers the differences in hoisting behavior between var and let, as well as the concept of the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ). This article is perfect for those preparing for a front-end interview or wanting to brush up on their JavaScript knowledge.",href:"/en/swe/hoisting"}]},{title:"Array",links:[{title:"What array traversal methods are there in JavaScript? (for loop, for...in, for...of, forEach, map, filter, every, some)",date:"2023-01-23",lastmod:"2023-02-01",toc:"javascript",lang:"en",fileName:"iterate-array",filePath:"src/pages/en/swe/iterate-array.md",fullPath:"/en/swe/iterate-array",subtoc:"array",ord